  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 156 PM MDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nAs we start off the long term period, the region will be sitting \nfirmly under a trough aloft with a northerly flow regime overhead. \nShifting gears a bit to the surface, there will be a deep surface \nlow located to our northeast across southeast South Dakota. The \nresult of the placement of this surface feature will be a tight \nnorthwest pressure gradient over the CWA causing surface winds to \nbecome northerly to northwesterly along with residual moisture being \nfed into the CWA. This will keep chances of precipitation, possibly \na few snow showers mixing in to our west across the Laramie Range \nand later in the evening across the Nebraska Panhandle with little \nto no snow accumulations expected through Monday night at lower \nelevations. Higher elevations, north of 9,000 feet could see some \naccumulating snowfall on the order of 1 to 3 additional inches \nMonday afternoon/evening, especially across the Sierra Madre and \nSnowy Ranges. Overall, QPF totals through Monday night look to be on \nthe order of a tenth to two tenths, highest across northern portions \nof the Nebraska Panhandle, of an inch primarily north of I-80 and \neast of I-25. As mentioned briefly above, the surface gradient will \ntighten and this will cause the potential for strong winds Monday \nnight in the wake of the surface low and associated cold front. \nEnsemble guidance supports to a degree wind gusts approaching or \nexceeding 50 mph, primarily a swath along the I-80 corridor in the \nNebraska Panhandle. Due to weak subsidence, Omega (GFS), these \nstrong winds will be gradient driven at the surface due to the tight \npressure gradient that sets up over the area.\n\nAs we progress in this forecast period, the stronger winds will \ndiminish through Tuesday morning into the early afternoon hours as \nthe surface low slides off to the east, causing the surface gradient \nto lax and winds to ease. Not only this, but drier conditions return \nwith weak ridging building in aloft by Tuesday afternoon. By \nWednesday, some model differences arise with a trough that moves to \nour west and how that feature evolves. One solution has it a bit \nfaster and sliding across the CWA through Wednesday while the EC has \nit a bit slower and weaker. Beyond Wednesday, model consensus lacks \nwith the placement and amplitude of synoptic features, so stay tuned \nto the forecast for future refinements to the forecast. Now, taking \na closer look at temperatures, expect highs to climb into the lower \nto mid 40s west of I-25 and lower to mid 50s east of I-25 through \nthe remainder of the work week into next weekend. While lows dip \ninto the lower to mid 30s across the CWA, coldest west of the I-25 \ncorridor.",
